   "html_text": "When we think of evolutionary theory and natural selection, we usually think of, say, a herd of deer, in which some deer are faster than others escaping their predators. These swifter deer will, all things being equal, (on average) leave more offspring, and these offspring will tend to be swifter than other deer. Thus, we get a change in the average swiftness of deer over evolutionary time. In a case like this, the “unit of selection”, often called the “target” of selection, is (at least) the si",
